---
source: crates/cli/tests/cli.rs
expression: result.stdout
---
Run test cases against rules

Usage: cleanrules test [OPTIONS] <RULES> [CASES]...

Arguments:
  <RULES>
          The rules: a rule set, decision table, bundle or rule, or - for standard input

  [CASES]...
          Case files (.json, .yaml, .yml), or - for standard input; without them, a bundle runs the case files it lists

Options:
      --syntax <SYNTAX>
          The syntax of the rules; by default, from their file's suffix (.json, .yaml, .yml, .rules, .csv), and json for standard input

          Possible values:
          - json: JSON
          - yaml: YAML
          - text: The rule syntax
          - csv:  CSV, for decision tables

      --format <FORMAT>
          What to write

          Possible values:
          - text: Text for people
          - json: The JSON document, on one line
          
          [default: text]

      --max-results <N>
          Stop the run after this many results (run limit max_results)

      --max-steps <N>
          Stop the run before it takes more steps, one per rule and case (run limit max_steps)

      --max-explained <N>
          Explain at most this many failed cases (run limit max_explained) [default: 5]

      --ruleset-digest
          Carry the rule set digest of the rules tested in the JSON

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Exit codes:
  0  done, nothing found at or above --fail-on; for test, every case ran and
     passed
  1  findings at or above --fail-on (audit), a document that does not read
     (validate), or a case that failed or erred, a partial run or no cases
     (test)
  2  usage error
  3  a file could not be read or written, a document did not read, or the
     operation is not supported for it

Specification: https://github.com/cleanrules/cleanrules/blob/main/spec/cli.md
